WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.557
Coding Exercise 5 keeps every employee row and adds a salary rank within each department.

00:00:06.557 --> 00:00:11.552
Because the detail rows must remain, do not collapse the table with GROUP BY.

00:00:11.552 --> 00:00:13.662
Use a ranking window function.

00:00:13.662 --> 00:00:19.287
The partition should separate departments, and the ordering should place the highest salary first.

00:00:19.287 --> 00:00:22.417
Run and Check, then scan each department.

00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:26.762
The highest-paid employee in each department should have rank one.

00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:31.212
If several departments all have a rank-one employee, that is correct.

00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:34.282
The ranking restarts because of the partition.

00:00:34.282 --> 00:00:40.047
This exercise is testing whether you can recognize when analytical context belongs in a window function rather

00:00:40.047 --> 00:00:42.202
than in a grouped summary.

00:00:42.202 --> 00:00:46.032
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:46.032 --> 00:00:51.107
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:51.107 --> 00:00:54.637
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:58.992
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:06.077
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:12.447
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:14.734
not only whether the statement executes.
